---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { units } from '../lib/units';
const volumeUnits = units.filter(unit => unit.category === 'volume');
---
<BaseLayout title="How We Calculate Unit Prices | Which Deal's Better?" description="The calculator's actual formulas, supported conversions, count handling, five-deal limit, tie tolerance, rounding rules, and local processing." allowAds>
<section class="content-section"><article class="shell prose">
<p class="eyebrow">Calculation methodology</p><h1>How we calculate</h1>
<p>The <a href="/">Which Deal's Better? calculator</a> compares the price of a shared amount of product. It uses the prices, quantities, and units you enter to identify the lowest unit cost. It does not look up store prices or decide whether products have the same quality. This page describes the implemented calculation and display rules, including limits that matter when interpreting a result.</p>

<h2>Price divided by total quantity</h2>
<p>For a single package, the calculation is Price ÷ Amount. A $6 package containing 24 ounces has a unit price of $0.25 per ounce. Price refers to the amount paid for all the contents represented by that deal.</p>
<p>When “This is a multipack” is enabled, total quantity is “Number of items in pack” multiplied by “Amount per item.” A $12 bundle containing four 500 mL bottles has 2,000 mL in total, so its underlying unit price is $0.006 per mL. With the multipack option off, the package multiplier is one.</p>
<p class="formula">Normalized quantity = number of items × quantity per item × conversion factor</p>
<p class="formula">Underlying unit price = price ÷ normalized quantity</p>
<p>Prices and amounts must be finite numbers greater than zero. The number of items in a multipack must also be a whole number. A zero-priced standalone deal is not accepted; for a free item within a paid promotion, enter the total paid for the offer and include the free item's quantity. Every visible deal needs valid details before a comparison is shown.</p>

<h2>Compatible measurement groups</h2>
<p>The calculator converts quantities to a shared base within a supported group. It compares U.S. weight, metric weight, volume, count, U.S. length, and metric length as six separate groups. A unit's conversion factor represents how many base units it contains.</p>
<h3>Weight</h3>
<p>Ounces and pounds share an ounce base: 1 pound = 16 ounces. Grams and kilograms share a gram base: 1 kilogram = 1,000 grams. U.S. and metric weight remain separate in this implementation. An ounce deal and a gram deal trigger the incompatible-units message; the calculator does not automatically convert between those groups.</p>
<h3>Volume</h3>
<p>All supported volume units share a milliliter base. Fluid ounces, cups, pints, quarts, and gallons use U.S. liquid measures, not Imperial measures. They can be compared with milliliters and liters. The factors below come directly from the calculator's unit definitions.</p>
<dl class="conversions">{volumeUnits.map(unit => <div><dt>1 {unit.longLabel}</dt><dd>{unit.factor} mL</dd></div>)}</dl>
<p>A weight ounce and a fluid ounce belong to different groups. The calculator does not use product density to convert weight to volume. Check the label before choosing oz or fl oz.</p>
<h3>Count</h3>
<p>Item, piece, pod, tablet, capsule, roll, sheet, bag, bottle, and can all have a factor of one in the same count group. The calculator therefore accepts them as numerically comparable counts. It does not infer that a roll contains a particular number of sheets or that a bottle holds a particular volume.</p>
<p>Choose a meaningful common count yourself. To compare six rolls with 120 sheets each against eight rolls with 80 sheets each, use sheet as Unit and enter the roll count and sheets per roll in the multipack fields. Comparing roll with sheet directly would be accepted as a count comparison but would not represent equivalent amounts of paper.</p>
<h3>Length</h3>
<p>Inches, feet, and yards share an inch base: 1 foot = 12 inches and 1 yard = 36 inches. Centimeters and meters share a centimeter base: 1 meter = 100 centimeters. U.S. and metric length are separate groups, so feet and meters are not converted automatically.</p>
<p>The calculator does not adjust for width, thickness, or area. Price per yard can be useful for comparable ribbon, but a wider roll may contain more material for the same length.</p>

<h2>Incompatible and incomplete comparisons</h2>
<p>If valid deals use different groups, the result says “Units don't match” and does not choose a winner. Individual card prices may still appear, but they are not a valid cross-group comparison. Choose compatible units or convert the package details yourself before entering them.</p>
<p>Blank or invalid fields instead prompt you to check the details. The interface starts with two deals and allows up to five, labeled A through E. Remove an unwanted extra deal or complete it so it does not prevent comparison.</p>

<h2>Lowest price, ties, and the next-best deal</h2>
<p>The lowest unrounded normalized unit price determines the best deal. Results remain listed in their original A–E order; the calculator identifies the best price without rearranging the cards into a sorted ranking. With one winner, it highlights that card and compares its unit price with the next-lowest unit price.</p>
<p class="formula">Advantage (%) = (next-lowest price − lowest price) ÷ next-lowest price × 100</p>
<p>For unit prices of $0.15, $0.20, and $0.30, the advantage is 25% relative to $0.20. It is not measured against the most expensive option or against the difference in package checkout totals.</p>
<p>A tiny tolerance prevents floating-point arithmetic from separating effectively equal lowest prices. A deal ties for best when its distance from the lowest normalized unit price is no greater than the larger of lowest price × 0.000000001 and JavaScript's Number.EPSILON. If multiple deals meet that condition, the result shows a tied-best message and no unique winner. Other listed deals can still have higher prices; a tie does not imply that every entry is equal.</p>

<h2>Displayed prices and rounding</h2>
<p>Each deal card displays its price in that card's chosen unit. The comparison list and mobile result bar use the first deal's unit for all prices. Changing that first unit can change the scale of the displayed numbers without changing which underlying unit cost is lowest.</p>
<p>Prices are formatted as U.S. dollars with two decimal places and rounded <strong>upward</strong> to the next cent, rather than to the nearest cent. For example, an underlying $0.0625 per fl oz displays as $0.07/fl oz. A small floating-point adjustment avoids moving an exact-cent value upward solely because of arithmetic noise.</p>
<p>The rounding is for display only. Two prices that both display as $0.07 can still have a unique winner because their unrounded values differ. Very small per-gram or per-milliliter prices make this especially noticeable. Selecting kilograms or liters for the comparison can make the displayed difference easier to see.</p>
<p>The advantage percentage uses up to one decimal place when it is below 1%, and otherwise uses no decimal places. A very small advantage can therefore display as 0% even when the underlying prices are not tied.</p>

<h2>Local calculations and the limits of the result</h2>
<p>The calculator processes entries in your browser. Its calculation code does not send those entries to a server or save them to persistent browser storage. No account is required. Analytics and advertising, where configured, are separate site services; see the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> for their context. Submitting the contact form is a separate action that sends the form information.</p>
<p>The calculation does not automatically apply coupons, taxes, promotions, waste estimates, or cost-per-use adjustments. Enter the price and usable comparison amount appropriate to your question. For practical entries, see <a href="/guides/how-to-compare-unit-prices/">how to compare unit prices</a> and <a href="/guides/multipacks-bogo-multibuy/">multipacks and promotional bundles</a>, or return to the <a href="/">calculator</a> with your package details.</p>
</article></section>
</BaseLayout>
